  "text": "It matters, but not in a way that ruins dinner! Freshly cooked rice has much more moisture than day-old rice, which can lead to \"mushy\" or clumpy fried rice if you aren't careful.\n\nHere\u2019s how to adapt your technique to get great results with fresh rice:\n\n### \ud83d\uded1 Critical Step: Dry It Out\n1. **Spread it out:** Immediately after cooking, spread the hot rice on a large baking sheet or plate in a thin layer.\n2. **Cool it down:** Let it sit at room temperature for 15\u201320 minutes. If you have time, put it in the fridge uncovered (or loosely covered) for another 15\u201330 minutes. This lets steam escape and firms up the grains.\n\n### \ud83d\udd25 Adjusted Cooking Method\nSince the rice is wetter, you need to cook hotter and faster to avoid steaming instead of frying.\n\n1. **Scramble eggs first** in a very hot pan with oil/butter. Remove them immediately so they don\u2019t overcook.\n2. **Saut\u00e9 onion** until soft (about 2\u20133 mins). Add frozen peas directly from frozen\u2014they\u2019ll thaw quickly.\n3. **Add rice.** Break up any clumps with your spatula. Stir-fry vigorously for 2\u20133 minutes to evaporate excess moisture. You want to hear sizzling, not simmering.\n4. **Return eggs** to the pan.\n5. **Season with soy sauce.** Drizzle it around the edges of the pan, not directly onto the rice, to prevent burning. Toss everything together for just 1 minute.\n\n### \ud83d\udca1 Pro Tips\n- **Don\u2019t overcrowd the pan.** If your pan is small, cook in two batches. Crowding = steaming = mushy rice.\n- **High heat is key.** Get your pan smoking-hot before adding anything.\n- **Optional upgrade:** If you have butter, add a knob at the end for richness. If you have garlic or ginger, mince some into the onions.\n\nYou\u2019ve got this! Even with fresh rice, you can make delicious, fluffy fried rice if you dry it out first and cook it hot and fast.",
